01:03.140 --> 01:07.220
So how many rows we can like insert inside each packet.

01:07.460 --> 01:09.100
So the calculation is very simple.

01:09.100 --> 01:15.460
It says the packet size equals to the number of rows divided by the number of packets.

01:15.660 --> 01:17.060
So what is the number of rows.

01:17.060 --> 01:18.980
Here we have four rows right.

01:19.140 --> 01:20.940
So we have four over here.

01:21.180 --> 01:24.620
Then the number of packets we define it in the syntax of the query.

01:24.780 --> 01:26.300
So here we defined two packets.

01:26.300 --> 01:27.540
We need two groups.

01:27.540 --> 01:30.540
So that means we are dividing four by two.

01:30.700 --> 01:32.620
And the size of the bucket is going to be two.

01:32.660 --> 01:36.420
So now with this SQL is ready I'm going to start assigning each row to a packet.

01:36.420 --> 01:37.700
So it's going to start with the top.

01:37.700 --> 01:40.100
The first one is going to be in the packet number one.

01:40.140 --> 01:41.460
Then go to the next one.

01:41.460 --> 01:45.180
It's going to say okay we still have enough space in the packet right.

01:45.180 --> 01:47.180
So it's going to assign it as well to one.

01:47.180 --> 01:51.700
But with this we reach the maximum number of rows within each packets.

01:51.700 --> 01:54.460
So the next row is going to be assigned to another bucket.

01:54.460 --> 01:55.580
So it's going to be two.

01:55.980 --> 01:57.820
And the last one is going to be as well two.

01:57.820 --> 01:57.840
too.

01:57.880 --> 01:59.400
So as you can see, it's very simple.

01:59.400 --> 02:03.800
We have just assigned our sales based on the sorting of course, into two buckets.

02:03.920 --> 02:06.360
These two sales belongs to the bucket number one.

02:06.360 --> 02:09.200
And the other two belongs to the bucket number two.

02:09.240 --> 02:09.800
Very easy.

02:09.800 --> 02:12.720
So that was very straightforward because we are dividing even numbers.

02:12.720 --> 02:15.000
And we got perfectly sized buckets.

02:15.000 --> 02:17.840
But now what can happen if we have an odd number.

02:17.840 --> 02:20.400
So we have here five instead of four.

02:20.720 --> 02:23.400
So the bucket size is going to be dividing five by two.

02:23.440 --> 02:24.920
We're going to get 2.5.

02:24.960 --> 02:29.080
And now of course SQL will not go and divide like two half for each bucket.

02:29.080 --> 02:31.120
Then we are splitting this into two buckets.

02:31.160 --> 02:32.920
Of course this will not be working.

02:32.960 --> 02:36.720
We should have now a bucket with three and another bucket with two.

02:36.760 --> 02:39.200
So now the rule in SQL make it very clear.

02:39.200 --> 02:43.000
It says larger groups comes first then smaller.

02:43.000 --> 02:48.280
So that means if we have here an even number like this, the larger group going to be the first group.

02:48.480 --> 02:50.480
So that's going to look like this.

02:50.520 --> 02:52.920
It's going to like reset everything.

02:53.480 --> 02:55.120
So let's see what's going to happen.

02:55.120 --> 02:56.820
The first one going to be one.

02:56.860 --> 02:58.420
The second one as Bill one.

02:58.660 --> 02:59.820
The third one going to be.

02:59.860 --> 03:00.820
As well one.

03:00.820 --> 03:04.660
So it kind of has a larger package than the second one.

03:04.980 --> 03:06.660
Then there is going to be two.

03:06.900 --> 03:10.820
So as you can see the larger group comes first then the smaller.

03:10.820 --> 03:13.700
And this is how SQL can work if you have odd numbers.

03:13.700 --> 03:20.100
So you don't have here perfectly sized buckets, you have approximately or roughly equal sized buckets.

03:20.100 --> 03:21.660
So this is how the install works.

